{"kind":"request","text":"Design: how a register retires a founder\n\nScope: from 662fec93 and 40ea8fdf. An operator learns that a key on a register's founders list was stolen. Today nothing removes it. `founders` is fixed in `packages/platform/src/register.ts`, `install` alone writes it, and the key can keep signing `found`, which opens claims and starts creating repositories on that register. This matters on a register under policy `keys`. Under policy `open`, any key can already sign `found`.\n\nWrite a design note. Open with that scene: who notices, what they do, and what they see afterward. Then answer:\n- Which key may retire a founder, and what happens when the stolen key is the only founder.\n- What happens to the claims and repository creations that the stolen key started, including those in progress when the key is retired.\n- The change to the register's definition and its pin. No backward compatibility is needed.\n- Whether an operator on a hosted Worker needs anything beyond that act.\n\nDone when the note is approved by the checker and landed. The note's status says that no code is built. It needs no deployment.\n\nOrder: below the builder's current items.","body":{"conditions":"A design note on retiring a register founder is approved by the checker and landed on main.","target_head":"8f129cdf83c36b059adb0a4fe071da9bb7916fa8","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}